## 5. Economy ✅ STAGE 5 COMPLETE

### Income sources (MVP)

| Source | Track | Risk | Setup Cost | Skill Ceiling | Time Cost | Day-fill role |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Weed growing | Dealing | Low | Medium | Medium | Slow (multi-day cycles) | Background maintenance |
| **Pager dealing** | Dealing | Medium | None (after first crop) | Medium-high | Variable, schedule-disrupting | **The schedule engine** |
| Car looting | Criminal | Medium | Low (slim jim eventually) | Low-medium | Fast (seconds per car) | Filler / opportunistic |
| Chop shop deliveries (late) | Criminal | High | Med (skills) | High | Medium (delivery walks) | Big-payoff session activity |

The two tracks (Dealing, Criminal) mirror each other structurally. Each has an XP spine (DealerExperience, CriminalExperience), each has tier-gated content, each unlocks tools/contacts/locations as it progresses. The player can lean into either, both, or alternate.

### Wallet

Multi-pool by design. MVP only uses `"cash"`. The pool architecture supports future expansion to `"clean"` (banked/checked) for catalogue mail-order, plus laundering bookkeeping. All Wallet methods take an optional pool parameter so the expansion is additive.

Starting cash: $50. Weekly rent: $100. Raw bud base value: $15 (Fence pays $9 wholesale, retail to dealing customers pays $22.50).

### Expenses
- **Rent:** weekly, due Sunday (auto-deducted). 2 misses = eviction (MVP: logged signal only; future: shanty-town tent fallback)
- **Food:** stamina maintenance — skip too long and tomorrow's stamina cap drops (deferred, post-MVP polish)
- **Supplies:** soil, seeds, lockpicks, baggies, etc.
- **Gifts:** social currency
- **Bribes / fines:** when caught
- **Equipment upgrades:** grow lamps, planter beds, tools (see below)

### Vendor system

One `VendorInteractable` node type supports both buying and selling via two multipliers:

- `buy_multiplier` — applied to ItemDef.base_value when player buys from vendor
- `sell_multiplier` — applied when player sells to vendor

Setting `sell_multiplier = 0` makes a vendor buy-only (e.g., hardware store). The VendorPanel UI handles both directions transparently — click your inventory to stage sells, click their stock to stage buys, see live net, confirm.

**Owner-NPC gating (new).** A vendor can declare a `vendor_owner: StringName` matching an NPC id. While the owner is materialized in the same room (via NPCDirector — see §11.5), the vendor's interactable is active. When the owner isn't present, the counter is dim/disabled. Vendors with `vendor_owner == &""` are always-on (vending machines, dead-drops, etc.). This is the clean way to express "store closed when the shopkeeper isn't there" without duplicating schedule logic on the vendor.

**Configured vendors:**
- **The Fence** — buys only. `sell_multiplier = 0.6` (wholesale). Empty stock. No owner.
- **Hardware Store** — sells only. `buy_multiplier = 1.0`. Stocks pots, soil, seeds. Owner: `oliver`.

When pawn shop and catalogue arrive (post-MVP), they'll be the same node type with different stock and multipliers.

### Pawn shop / catalogue (deferred, architected)

Two channels for acquiring equipment, each with different inventory and friction:

- **Pawn shop** (in-person, cash): rotating daily stock. Basics always available. Mid-tier items appear at higher DealerExperience tiers — the Fence stocks what makes sense for your level of operation.
- **Mail-order catalogue** (order + wait): higher-tier equipment. Player sends a check (physical cash committed), waits 2-3 days for delivery. Creates a planning horizon and a reason to check the mailbox.

DealerExperience gates progression in both channels. More XP → more contacts → better stock.

### Dirty vs. clean cash

Architected via the multi-pool wallet but inert for MVP. The catalogue system, when it ships, will use a `"clean"` pool that requires physical bank visits to load. Money laundering loop is post-MVP.

