## 5.6. Gift Giving ✅ COMPLETE

Stardew-style relationship currency. Player gives NPCs items, receives an affinity reaction graded by category. One special "perfect" gift per NPC unlocks event flags for story-gated content.

### Tiers and deltas

Six tiers, global config on GiftSystem autoload:

- **HATE** (−40 affinity)
- **DISLIKE** (−20)
- **NEUTRAL** (0) — fallback for any category not declared in an NPC's preferences
- **LIKE** (+20)
- **LOVE** (+80)
- **PERFECT** (+200) — declared on the ItemDef, not the NPC

Default reaction lines live on GiftSystem (`"Why would you give me this?"` for HATE, `"Thanks."` for NEUTRAL, etc.). Per-NPC overrides authored in the GiftPreferences .tres file.

### Authoring

**Per-NPC preferences:** `res://data/gifts/<npc_id>.tres`, a `GiftPreferences` Resource. Four typed arrays of ItemDef.Category — `like_categories`, `love_categories`, `dislike_categories`, `hate_categories`. Anything not in any list defaults to NEUTRAL. Optional per-tier line overrides as multiline strings. This is the only system using .tres for content — preferences are too small to warrant a parser, and the inspector affords clean dropdown authoring.

**Perfect gift:** declared on the ItemDef itself via `perfect_gift_for: StringName`. Empty = no perfect target. Giving the named NPC this specific item beats category lookup, fires `perfect_gift_given` signal, and sets the global flag `<npc_id>_perfect_gift_received` for event scenes to gate on. Triggering the cutscene is the event system's job, not the gift system's — the flag is the handoff.

**ItemDef.giftable:** boolean field, default true. Set false on tools, keys, and quest items to hide them from the gift panel.

### Category redesign

The original `Category` enum (TOOL/SEED/MATERIAL/CONSUMABLE/GIFT/KEY/MISC) was inventory-facing only. Extended with gift-side categories: FOOD, DRINK, FLOWER, JEWELRY, ENTERTAINMENT, CLOTHING, DECORATION, DRUG. One flat tag set — same enum drives inventory filtering and gift grading. CONSUMABLE and GIFT are retained as legacy values for backwards-compatibility but not used for new items.

**Migration note:** the new categories were appended to preserve existing .tres data. Re-categorizing pre-existing items (e.g., weed_buds → DRUG) is content work, not migration code.

### Daily cooldown

One gift per NPC per in-game day. Tracked on RelationshipSystem via `_last_gift_day: Dictionary` (npc_id → day_index). Resets on day rollover automatically (no expiry logic — just `last_day != today`). NPC interaction silently rejects the gift panel open with a notification line when the cooldown is active.

This also sets the precedent for general daily NPC interaction caps (e.g., one conversation per day per NPC outside of events / gifts / quest turn-ins). Implementation deferred until needed.

### Flow

1. Player presses alt_interact near NPC (handled via InteractionManager.try_alt_interact — see §11.5)
2. NPC checks `RelationshipSystem.can_receive_gift(npc_id)` — fails fast with a notification if cooldown is active
3. GiftPanel opens, listing only items where `item.giftable == true` from player inventory
4. Player clicks a stack → one item consumed → `GiftSystem.give_gift(npc_id, item)` resolves the tier, applies the affinity delta, records the cooldown, emits signals
5. Panel closes, inline reaction dialogue plays via DialogueRuntime
6. For perfect gifts, the global flag is set — event scenes gated on `<npc_id>_perfect_gift_received` can fire next time their other conditions are satisfied

### Why not auto-trigger the perfect cutscene

The gift can be given anywhere on the map. Teleporting the player into a cutscene immediately would be jarring. The flag-and-wait pattern lets the cutscene fire naturally next time the player walks into the relevant room — same trigger model as every other event.

