## 11.5. NPC Schedules ✅ FRAMEWORK COMPLETE (CONTENT IN PROGRESS)

The substrate that makes the world feel populated. Each named NPC has a schedule — a time-keyed map of "where are you and what are you doing." As time advances, the schedule tells the NPC where to be, and the world materializes them when the player is there to see them.

### Materialize-on-room-enter

NPCs only physically exist when the player is in the scene the NPC's current schedule entry points to. Otherwise the NPC is a virtual entity tracked by `NPCDirector`. This is the same pattern proven by `MeetingSpawner` — meeting buyers materialize when the player enters the meet spot's room.

When the player enters a scene, NPCDirector iterates its registered NPCs and asks for each one: "does your current schedule entry point at this scene?" If yes, instantiate at the named Waypoint. If no, ensure no stale instance is hanging around. Same logic runs on every minute tick, on every room change, and on every schedule hot-reload — so the world is always reconciled to "what the schedule says right now."

### Cross-scene transit gap

Transit happens *inside one scene*. An NPC going from `oliver_house` → `ext_central` (the street) → `hardware_store` has three separate schedule entries: a stationary at home, a transit on the street, a stationary at the store. The first-to-second and second-to-third transitions are simultaneous — at minute X he's in the house, at minute X he's on the street. From the player's perspective: if they're watching the front door at minute X they see him appear on the street side. If they're watching the bodega the moment he disappears from home, they see nothing.

This is acceptable because rooms have visual boundaries (walls, doors), and the player can't see "between" rooms. The illusion holds as long as schedule entries are authored coherently with the level geometry.

### Activities

Two structural kinds of schedule entry:

- **Stationary** — NPC stands at a named waypoint, optionally facing a direction, playing a named animation. The animation name is arbitrary data: `stand`, `sit`, `smoke`, `work`, `sleep` — whatever's authored in the SpriteFrames. Falls back to `idle` if not found. Adding a new activity is just adding the animation to the character's frames.
- **Transit** — NPC walks a chain of 2+ waypoints in one scene. Duration is implicit — the gap to the next schedule entry. Position interpolates per frame along the Manhattan-distance line, with constant apparent speed across all legs (so a chain of waypoints doesn't speed up at short segments). Facing flips per leg based on dominant axis.

Transit entries declare their walking animation via a `walk:NAME` prefix. `walk:walk` plays `walk_<facing>`; `walk:run` plays `run_<facing>` (for a hurried character); `walk:limp` plays `limp_<facing>` (post-injury); etc. Same animation-as-data principle as stationary.

### Speed-as-data (emergent design lever)

Because transit duration is implicit (`end_minute - start_minute`), authoring "Oliver leaves home at 08:45 and arrives at the store by 09:00" produces a faster walk than "leaves at 08:30 and arrives at 09:00." Same route, same arrival, different perceived urgency. This emerged from the implementation but turned out to be a useful authoring lever — characters in a hurry vs. sauntering vs. drunk-stumbling fall out of timing tuning, not separate code paths.

### Authored waypoints

Each scene with scheduled NPCs has a `Waypoints` Node2D child. `Waypoint` markers under it carry a `waypoint_id: StringName`. Schedule entries reference these ids. Naming convention: by location, not per-NPC. `home_door`, `store_door`, `corner_north`, `alley_west_bend` are shared across the cast. Per-NPC waypoints (`oliver_specific_spot`) are reserved for cases where it really is just one character's location.

Helper waypoints at every grid corner are encouraged. The transit system walks straight lines between consecutive waypoints, so any time a route would need to turn (avoid geometry, follow a sidewalk), insert a corner waypoint. This is much cheaper than real pathfinding and stays under authoring control.

### NavigationAgent2D — still deferred

Real pathfinding (`NavigationAgent2D` against a `NavigationRegion2D`) is *not* used by scheduled NPCs. They follow authored waypoint chains exclusively. The reactive-pathfinding tool earns its weight when pursuit cops chase the player (Stage 12), where the destination is dynamic. Until then, the navigation region authoring overhead isn't justified.

### Schedule priority vs. state machine (forward-looking)

When pursuit cops land in Stage 12, their state machine takes priority over their schedule. Reggie might miss his 13:00 precinct shift if he's still chasing the player, but he'll resume at the next entry that hasn't started yet. The schedule is what he "would be doing" — pursuit overrides it. NPCDirector queries schedule + checks state-machine override; the override layer is a Stage 12 extension.

### Architecture

- **`Schedule` resource** — thin wrapper around the `entries: Dictionary` (minute-of-day → entry dict). Provides `entry_at(minute)` and `entry_window_at(minute)` (the latter returns start/end minutes for transit interpolation). Wraps midnight automatically.
- **`ScheduleDatabase` autoload** — loads `.sched` files from `res://data/schedules/`, indexes scene basenames against `res://scenes/rooms/`, evaluates which schedule block is active for a given NPC under current context. Hot-reloads on file change (debug builds); F6 force-reloads. Mirrors `DialogueDatabase`.
- **`NPCDirector` autoload** — owns NPC registration (scene + display_name). Per minute tick / room change, queries ScheduleDatabase for each NPC's current entry, reconciles scene tree, instantiates/despawns. Public API: `is_npc_present(npc_id)`, `is_npc_in_scene(npc_id, scene_path)`, `get_current_entry(npc_id)`. Other systems (vendors, doors) gate on these.
- **`ScheduledNPC`** — extends `NPC` (which owns dialogue/Interactable/sprite). Adds `set_stationary` / `set_transit` methods that NPCDirector calls during reconciliation. Per-frame `_update_transit_pose` for the interpolated walk.
- **`Waypoint`** — Node2D with `waypoint_id`. Authored under each scene's `Waypoints` container.

### Open questions

- Schedule swap on event: works (`event_done X` precondition picks a different block), but the in-room transition is "snap to new pose" — no walk-out-of-old-pose animation. Probably fine for now.
- Long-term schedule evolution (NPC moves to a new apartment after Story Beat): handled by adding a `relocated` flag and gating blocks on it. Pattern works; just an authoring discipline thing.
- Reggie's patrol routes will likely want a "loop forever" transit pattern (walk a beat repeatedly until shift ends). Current authoring doesn't have first-class support, but you can emulate it with many short transit entries. May want a `loop:` activity prefix eventually.

