## 11.6. .sched File Format ✅ COMPLETE

Schedules are authored in `res://data/schedules/<npc_id>.sched`. Filename basename = npc_id. Format mirrors `.dlg` closely (same key/precondition syntax, same hot-reload, same lookup model). Hot-reload on save in debug builds; F6 force-reloads.

### Structure

A file is a list of **blocks**. Each block is one variant of a daily schedule, gated by tags + preconditions. At any moment the highest-specificity matching block wins, and its time-keyed entries drive the NPC.

```
KEY [| precondition ; precondition ; ...]
	HH:MM activity scene args
	HH:MM activity scene args
```

Body lines tab-indented.

### Keys

| Key | Specificity | Matches |
|---|---|---|
| `default` | -1 | nothing else matches |
| `*` | 0 | always (use with preconditions) |
| `mon`..`sun`, `weekday`, `weekend` | 1 | as named |
| `mon.rainy` | 2 | both tags present (AND) |

Tag matching is AND. Tie-breaking: same-specificity blocks are won by **file order — later wins**. Convention: place "patch" blocks (a block that should override another when its conditions are met) lower in the file.

### Preconditions

Same syntax as `.dlg`:

- `event_done X` — RelationshipSystem records this event as complete
- `has_item X` — player carries item with id X
- `flag_name` / `!flag_name` — NPC or global flag set / not set
- `knows` / `!knows` — RelationshipSystem.get_knows for this NPC
- Comparison ops on stats: `affinity > 40`, `trust < 20`, `day >= 5`, `dow == 2`
- Multiple preconditions separated by `;` (all must be true)

### Schedule lines

Two activity shapes:

```
HH:MM <animation> SCENE MARKER [FACING]              # stationary
HH:MM walk:<animation> SCENE A->B->C...              # transit
```

- `SCENE` is the room basename (no extension, no folder). ScheduleDatabase resolves it.
- `MARKER` is a waypoint id in that scene.
- `FACING` (stationary only) is `north`/`south`/`east`/`west`. Drives `<anim>_<facing>` selection. Default south.
- Transit routes are 2+ waypoint ids joined by `->`. All waypoints in one scene.
- Times are 24-hour calendar minute-of-day.

Stationary `<animation>` is any name — `stand`, `sit`, `smoke`, `sleep`, `work`. Falls back to `idle` if absent from SpriteFrames.

Transit `walk:<animation>` is the directional prefix — `walk:walk` → `walk_north/south/east/west`, `walk:run` → `run_north/...`, etc. Same fallback to `idle`.

### Time windows

Each entry occupies the window from its `HH:MM` until the next entry's `HH:MM`. Last entry wraps to first entry next day (so an evening "stand at home" covers overnight). Transit duration is the window's length — *speed = total Manhattan distance / window minutes, constant across the route*. Squeeze or expand the gap to control walking speed.

### Block selection

For each NPC, ScheduleDatabase iterates blocks, eliminates those whose tags aren't all in current context, eliminates those whose preconditions fail, picks highest specificity (later-in-file wins ties). Result is a `Schedule` wrapping just that block's entries. NPCDirector then queries `entry_window_at(now)` against it.

Current context tags include: weekday name, `weekday`/`weekend`, all global flags from RelationshipSystem, all NPC-specific flags. Forthcoming: weather, time-of-day band — plumb in when those systems land.

### Authoring patterns

**Event-driven variant** — patch a default with a gated precondition:

```
default
	09:00 stand hardware_store counter south

default | event_done bodega_incident
	11:00 stand hardware_store counter south
```

Default applies pre-event; the gated default (same specificity, later in file) takes over post-event.

**Weekend schedule:**

```
sat
	10:00 stand park bench_a east
	14:00 walk:walk ext_central park_exit->store_door
	14:30 stand hardware_store counter south
```

**Late-for-work hustle** — short transit window = fast walk:

```
08:55 walk:run ext_central home_door->corner->store_door
09:00 stand hardware_store counter south
```

Five minutes for the whole walk = visible hurry.

### Gotchas

- Scene basenames must be unique across `res://scenes/rooms/`. Two `office.tscn` in different subfolders won't work — the index keys by basename.
- Transit waypoints all live in one scene. Cross-room walks are sequential transit entries in different scenes with implicit "in transit between" gaps.
- Marker ids are case-sensitive `StringName`s. Typo = silent warning + NPC fails to materialize at that entry.
- A block with failing preconditions just doesn't match — it doesn't disable other blocks. Always have a `default`.

