## 11.7. Scripted Events ✅ STAGE 11 COMPLETE

Authored cutscene system for story beats, introductions, and state-shift moments. Designed around editor-driven authoring — events are .tscn scenes, steps are nodes, beat timing is dragged into place visually.

### The "event is a room" model

An event scene is structurally a duplicated room (e.g., duplicate `hardware_store.tscn`, rename to `oliver_intro.tscn` in `res://scenes/events/`). Doorway interactables are stripped manually post-duplication so the player can't escape mid-cutscene. The duplicated room serves as the cutscene's stage — tilemaps, walls, props are real, lighting is real, the camera frame is whatever you point a Camera2D at in the event scene.

**Firing = changing rooms.** When an event's conditions pass, EventDirector calls `RoomManager.change_room(event_scene_path, "default")` under cover of a ScreenFade. The active scene becomes the event scene. On completion, change back to the originating room at the player's pre-event position. The handoff reuses the entire door-transition plumbing — fades, room state, NPCDirector reconciliation — for free.

**Player handling.** The real Player node is set to `PROCESS_MODE_DISABLED` for the event's duration, which freezes input, kills its camera, and hides the sprite all at once. Any on-screen player presence during a cutscene is a CutsceneActor puppet (see below). Restored to `PROCESS_MODE_INHERIT` after the room swap back, under cover of the closing fade.

### Scene structure
event_scene.tscn
├── (room tilemaps / walls / decorations, duplicated from source room)
├── Event           (Event node — id, conditions, repeatable)
├── Cast            (container)
│   ├── Oliver      (CutsceneActor)
│   ├── Player      (CutsceneActor — when player is on-screen)
│   └── Marker1     (Marker2D — movement target)
├── Steps           (container)
│   ├── Line1       (DialogueStep)
│   ├── Wait1       (WaitStep)
│   ├── Move1       (MoveStep)
│   ├── Line2       (DialogueStep)
│   └── Choice1     (ChoiceStep)
│       ├── Accept  (Branch)
│       │   └── Line_A1 (DialogueStep)
│       └── Decline (Branch)
│           └── Line_D1 (DialogueStep)
├── default         (Marker2D — RoomManager spawn point)
└── Camera2D        (frames the cutscene)

### Step nodes

All inherit `EventStep extends Node`. Director iterates `Steps.get_children()` and `await`s each `run(event_root)`. Synchronous steps return immediately; async steps suspend.

Shipped step types:

- **DialogueStep** — one line of dialogue. Speaker is a CutsceneActor NodePath; the dialogue box header pulls `dialogue_npc_id`, `display_name`, and `default_portrait` from the actor automatically. Multi-line scenes = multiple sequential DialogueSteps (one beat per step, easy to reorder).
- **ChoiceStep** — branching dialogue. Has child `Branch` nodes (one per outcome). Each Branch has a `label` (menu text) and EventStep children that run when picked. N-way branching for free; SceneTree order is menu order.
- **WaitStep** — real-time delay in seconds. Used for beat pacing between dialogue.
- **MoveStep** — tween-walks a CutsceneActor to a Marker2D's position. Picks `walk_<facing>` animation from the dominant axis of travel.
- **FaceStep** — instant facing change, plays the corresponding idle pose.
- **AnimationStep** — plays a named animation on a CutsceneActor, optionally awaits finish.
- **SetFlagStep** — sets a global or per-NPC flag via RelationshipSystem.
- **MarkEventDoneStep** — manual override to mark some other event done from within this one. The current event auto-marks done on completion via `repeatable = false`.

Step nodes are saved as reusable .tscn templates in `res://scenes/event_steps/` for drag-and-drop authoring. Adding a new step type = one new .gd file inheriting EventStep, no director changes.

### CutsceneActor

Puppet sprite, not a real NPC. Wraps an AnimatedSprite2D and exposes the methods steps need to drive it: `face(direction)`, `walk_to(target_position, speed)`, `play_animation(name, await_finish)`. Carries `dialogue_npc_id`, `display_name`, `default_portrait` for DialogueStep header lookup. Does not interact with NPCDirector, schedules, or any other NPC plumbing — the real NPC stays unrendered (or wherever its schedule had it) for the cutscene's duration.

**Design rationale:** the real NPC node has stamina, state, signal connections, and save serialization. Remote-controlling it for a cutscene is brittle. Puppets are dumb and predictable. The visual cost (slight pose discontinuity at handoff under the fade) is invisible in practice.

### Trigger model

Each Event node has:
- `id: String` — globally unique; matches filename convention
- `conditions: Array[String]` — precondition strings, syntax mirrors .dlg/.sched
- `repeatable: bool` (default false) — when false, EventDirector implicitly adds `!event_done <id>` to conditions and auto-marks done on completion
- `return_room_path` / `return_spawn` — optional override for where to return to after the event

EventDirector evaluates triggers on `TimeSystem.minute_tick` and `RoomManager.room_changed` (deferred one frame after room changes to let the world settle). First eligible event wins. One event runs at a time.

### Condition vocabulary

Strings parsed by EventConditionEvaluator (separate from DialogueRuntime's parser, but same syntax for the shared subset):

- `flag` / `!flag` — global flag presence/absence
- `event_done <id>` — RelationshipSystem flag check
- `npc_in_scene <npc_id>` — NPCDirector reports the NPC's current schedule entry is in the current room
- `has_item <item_id>` — stubbed for future use
- `<stat> OP <value>` — compare. Stats: `day`, `dow`, `hour`, `room` (basename). Operators: `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, `==`, `!=`

Per-NPC affinity/trust intentionally left out of event conditions — set a flag from a dialogue effect or SetFlagStep and gate on the flag. Cross-system coupling kept narrow.

### Authoring workflow

1. Duplicate the room scene the event takes place in. Rename, move to `res://scenes/events/`. Strip doorways and any interactables that shouldn't fire.
2. Add Event, Cast, Steps as child nodes. Set the Event node's id, conditions, repeatable.
3. Drop CutsceneActor instances under Cast, position them in the 2D viewport against the real room geometry. Set their `dialogue_npc_id` / `display_name` / `default_portrait`.
4. Drop Marker2Ds under Cast for movement targets.
5. Build the step list under Steps. Drag step .tscn templates in, set NodePath fields, drag to reorder.
6. Add a Camera2D at the root, position it to frame the scene. The real player's camera is gone during the event; this one becomes current.
7. Save. Walk into the conditions in-game.

NodePath fields on steps resolve relative to the step node itself (the Godot editor picker's native scoping), not relative to the event root. The `event_root` parameter on `run()` is kept for steps that need to look up siblings or global-to-event nodes.

### Lambda pitfall (worth flagging for future step authors)

GDScript lambdas capture value-type variables (int, float, bool) **by value**, not by reference. A pattern like:

```gdscript
var picked: int = -1
DialogueRuntime.response_selected.connect(func(idx): picked = idx)
```

…assigns to the lambda's local copy, not the outer `picked`. Outer var stays -1. Bit me writing ChoiceStep; resolved by using a member var + bound method pattern. Worth using that pattern for any step that needs to capture a signal result.

### Debug

- `EventDirector.force_fire(event_id)` ignores conditions and runs a specified event. Bound to F12 in player.gd as a hardcoded id during current testing — swap the string when working on a different event.
- `RelationshipSystem.mark_event_undone(event_id)` clears the done-flag for natural re-triggering on next room change.

A debug overlay listing all event eligibility states was considered and deferred — force_fire + mark_event_undone covers 90% of the authoring need.

### Not built (deferred until a real scene needs them)

- **CameraStep** — pan, zoom, follow. Wait until a multi-shot cutscene.
- **ScreenFadeStep** — mid-event fade for time-jumps or scene-internal cuts.
- **SFX / Music steps** — wait until audio is in.
- **AutoAdvanceDialogueStep / dialogue autoplay toggle** — visual-novel-style timed advance with no input. Likely a toggle on DialogueRuntime rather than a new step type.
- **Condition-based BranchStep** — branch on a flag instead of a dialogue choice. Probably unnecessary — author two events with mutually exclusive conditions and let trigger evaluation pick.
- **Save mid-event** — not supported by design. Saves only happen on day rollover (and debug button), and events don't span day boundaries.

### Integration with future systems

The flag-then-event pattern is the standard for any system that wants to fire a cutscene from an arbitrary world action: set a flag on success, author an event gated on the flag plus location/time conditions, cutscene fires the next time the player is in the right place. Used for: perfect gift unlocks (§5.6), and intended for: quest turn-ins, detective knock (§15.2), buyer relationship milestones, criminal-experience tier crossings.

