22. `ScreenFade` — must precede EventDirector (used for room-swap cover)
23. `GiftSystem` — depends on RelationshipSystem, TimeSystem
24. `EventDirector` — depends on RoomManager, TimeSystem, RelationshipSystem, NPCDirector, ScreenFade, DialogueRuntime

### Save/load timing trap

`load_state` order across autoloads can't be relied on. Systems that need to sync with world state after their data loads should `call_deferred` a sync method from their own `load_state`. Examples: `CarSpawner.load_state` calls `call_deferred("_materialize_cars_for_current_scene")`; `NPCDirector.load_state` calls `call_deferred("_resync_after_load")` because `room_changed` already fired and TimeSystem may not yet be loaded at that point.

### Vendor system as a reusable pattern

`VendorInteractable` + `Stock` Inventory child + `VendorPanel` UI = the pattern for any "swap items for currency" interaction. Same node type covers Fence (sell-only), Hardware Store (buy-only with owner gating), eventual pawn shop (both), eventual catalogue (buy with delay). The two multipliers + sellable flag on ItemDef handle every variation. The optional `vendor_owner` field couples the vendor's availability to an NPC's schedule via NPCDirector — no schedule code on the vendor itself.

### Spawner pattern

The "register markers in scenes, manager picks from them on a trigger" pattern is now used by four systems:
- `MeetSpot` + `MeetingManager` (one-off scheduled meetings)
- `PatrolWaypoint` + `PatrolSpawner` (recurring patrol routes, post-MVP)
- `CarSpawnPoint` + `CarSpawner` (daily-renewable spawns)
- `Waypoint` + `NPCDirector` (per-room markers referenced by schedule entries)

Future systems (item spawners, decoration spawners) should follow the same pattern.

### Material-on-room-enter

Pattern proven by `MeetingSpawner`, `CarSpawner`, and now `NPCDirector`. Generic shape:
1. Manager owns state about what should exist in which scene
2. Listens to `RoomManager.room_changed`
3. On room enter, queries own state for "what belongs here?" and instantiates
4. On room exit, despawns or persists state back

### ItemDef tool framework

Items can declare movement-tool behavior via two ItemDef fields:

- `tool_id: StringName` — empty for passive items; non-empty triggers a tool dispatch in player.gd when interact is pressed while the item is in the active slot
- `movement_speed: float` — speed when this tool is the active movement mode

Player owns `active_mode: StringName` and `active_mode_speed: float`. Entering a mode reads the speed from the item def (so future vehicles are just new `.tres` files, not new code constants). Exiting clears both. `_current_speed()` returns `active_mode_speed` if a mode is active, otherwise the walking calculation with athletics multiplier.

This is the framework for the skateboard (Athletics-track upgrade item). Future bikes / rollerblades / cars-as-fast-travel plug in the same way — new tool_id, new dispatch line in `_try_tool_interact`, new entry in `MOVEMENT_TOOL_SCENES` if the new mode is scene-restricted.

### Interactable + collision shape separation

### Alt-interact dispatch

`Interactable` defines two signal/method pairs: `interacted` / `interact()` for the primary action (E key), and `alt_interacted` / `alt_interact()` for the alternate action (F key). InteractionManager mirrors with `try_interact` and `try_alt_interact`. NPCs override `alt_interact` to open the gift panel; future interactables can use it for context-appropriate secondary actions (examine vs. take, talk vs. trade, etc.).

Player's alt_interact input goes through InteractionManager first, falling back to PlacementSystem.try_pickup_targeted when no interactable claims it. This preserves the placeable-pickup behavior in open space while letting interactables intercept when in range.

### Event scene pattern

A "ducked-out-of-the-world" pattern for any system that needs to take over the screen for an authored sequence. The event scene is a regular .tscn the RoomManager swaps to, the real player is disabled for the duration, puppet sprites do the on-screen work, the room state survives unchanged. Useful for cutscenes (built), arrest flows (future), interrogation scenes (future), any sequence that wants full directorial control. The director-as-room-mover pattern is more flexible than overlay UI for anything that needs the world's geometry to be the stage.

### Inline reaction dialogue pattern

Pattern proven by GiftSystem: a "transactional" interaction (gift given, deal closed, quest turned in) immediately triggers DialogueRuntime with a single-line entry rather than queuing dialogue for later. Matches Stardew's "give item, see immediate reaction" feel; preserves the existing pager-style dialogue queue for "react later" beats.

Implementation shape: caller constructs an entry dict with one body segment (`{kind: "text", portrait, text}`) and calls `DialogueRuntime.start(npc_id, display_name, entry)` directly. Bypasses DialogueDatabase. Used for: gift reactions (§5.6), intended for: deal-completion lines, quest-turn-in reactions, contextual interjections.

Lesson learned: don't bundle collision shape inside the reusable Interactable scene. The shape is per-object data that varies; the script and behavior are what gets reused. Parent scenes provide their own CollisionShape2D as a child of the Interactable instance (with Editable Children enabled — safe usage since we're adding new children, not modifying inherited ones).

### Y-sort and chunking

Outdoor scenes use a YSortedContent parent for everything dynamic (wall tops, decoration with height, NPCs, player). Floor and wall-base TileMapLayers stay outside the y-sort group as flat backgrounds. z_index manipulation is reserved for vault arc state (see §24).

