## 21. Build Status & Architecture Decisions

[Existing content retained. Key additions:]

### Stage 5 / 6 / 7 specific decisions:

**Vendor system uses shadow inventories for staged transactions.** When the panel opens, it duplicates both player and vendor inventories. The player manipulates shadows freely; on Confirm the wallet delta is computed and the shadows are written back. On Cancel the shadows are discarded. This is the cleanest way to support multi-item buy+sell in one transaction without complex undo logic.

**Pricing uses base_value + per-vendor multipliers.** Each ItemDef has `base_value` and `sellable` fields. Each vendor has `buy_multiplier` and `sell_multiplier`. Final price = base × multiplier × count. Items where `sellable=false` are refused by sell-anywhere vendors regardless of multiplier.

**Customer roster persists per-save, not per-playthrough.** Originally considered cross-playthrough persistence; rejected as it would require state outside the save file. Each save has its own customer roster, seeded with the same starter customer and growing differently as the player progresses.

**Pages have a waking-time deadline, not wall-clock deadline.** A page that arrives at 4 AM doesn't expire while you sleep — the deadline ticks down only during waking hours (06:00–14:00 sleep window is paused). This means a late-night page is still actionable when you wake up.

**Meeting schedules round to quarter-hours.** "Meet at 6:45" feels more natural than "Meet at 6:32." Auto-scheduler picks the meeting time, then quantizes to the nearest 15.

**Car spawning is lazy per-scene, not daily-batch.** Cars generate when the player first enters an outdoor scene each day, not all-at-once at dawn. This handles cross-scene scaling naturally — scenes the player never visits don't generate anything.

**Car state persistence keyed by car_id, not by scene + position.** Carries through save/load cleanly. Day rollover clears the whole `_car_states` dict; new cars get fresh IDs.

**Placement gated by `placement_allowed` group on rooms.** Apartment rooms are in the group; outdoor and hallway are not. The hallway exception was the original prompt that revealed the missing gate.

**Animated doors use two named animations (open, close) plus frame-0-as-rest convention.** Frame 0 of `open` is the closed visual; frame 0 of `close` is the open visual. Resting state uses appropriate animation + frame 0 + stop().

**Interactable scenes carry no CollisionShape2D.** The shape lives in the parent scene as a child of the Interactable instance (Editable Children enabled). This handles the bed/door/payphone/car cases that all wanted different collision footprints without per-scene script changes.

**Spawn point scene_path resolution prefers `owner.scene_file_path`, not `current_scene`.** A spawn point inside a sub-scene loaded under a world wrapper would otherwise report the wrapper's path. `owner` correctly points to the *room* the marker was authored in.

### Stage 9 specific decisions:

**Schedule data lives in `.sched` files, not in code.** Initial implementation hardcoded Oliver's schedule in NPCDirector. Moved to data-driven .sched files for the same reason dialogue is in .dlg files — hot-reload, authoring without recompile, content separable from systems. Format mirrors .dlg deliberately so both files feel familiar to author.

**Activity type is animation name, not enum.** Originally `activity: STAND | TRANSIT | SIT | SMOKE` enum. Realized the only structural distinction is "is this stationary or transit" (which affects how position resolves), and the rest is just "play this animation." Now the entry has `kind: stationary | transit` and an arbitrary `animation: String`. Adding a new authored animation (smoking, sleeping, working) needs zero code changes — just SpriteFrames updates and a .sched line that references the new name. Transit uses a `walk:NAME` prefix to encode the directional animation while staying within the same format.

**Transit speed is implicit from the schedule timing window.** Each transit entry's duration = the gap to the next entry. Speed = total Manhattan distance / window minutes. This emerged from the implementation but became a load-bearing design lever — characters in a hurry vs sauntering vs limping fall out of timing tuning, not separate speeds-per-NPC config.

**Vendors gate on owner-NPC presence, doors gate on owner-NPC schedule location.** The two are subtly different. Vendors care about "is the NPC currently materialized in this room" (presence) — works because vendors live in a specific room and the player only sees the gate effect when they're there. Doors care about "is the NPC scheduled to be home right now" (schedule lookup) — works even when the player is on the wrong side of the door and the owner isn't materialized anywhere. Two methods, two use cases: `NPCDirector.is_npc_present(id)` and `NPCDirector.is_npc_in_scene(id, scene_path)`.

**Door locking factors compose, they don't priority-order.** Hard-locked days override everything. Schedule hours and owner-presence are both unlock paths; whichever is true unlocks the door. Late-lockout modifies the owner-presence path only. This composition lets one door type express stores-with-hours, homes-without-hours, late-night-bars, holiday-closures — without subclassing.

**ItemDef gains tool_id / movement_speed for the movement-tool pattern.** Speed lives on the item, not in player constants. Skateboard is the first instance; bikes and other vehicles plug in the same way. Player owns `active_mode` state that survives slot changes (player swaps inventory item, mode persists until explicitly exited or auto-dismounted on room change).

**Vault tween coordination uses one tween with `set_parallel(true)` and `chain()` before the completion callback.** Original implementation used two parallel tweens for position and arc — they appeared to finish simultaneously but actually had nondeterministic per-frame ordering, causing residual writes after the completion callback. Single-tween-with-parallel-children + chained callback guarantees ordering: position and arc tick together, then callback fires once both are complete.

